Good direction overall, but I want some UX adjustments before we finalize and before you generate the copy-pasteable Perfection prompt.

UX changes:

1. Remove the per-question "If skipped:" lines.

They create a lot of repeated visual noise.

Instead, keep skip behavior under:

[OTHER ACTIONS AVAILABLE]

and explain it in plain language.

For example:

[OTHER ACTIONS AVAILABLE]

• Type your own answer
  Use your own wording instead of any option.

• Skip a question
  Perfection will continue, but that unanswered item may need to be provided later before the workflow can make certain decisions.

• Compile now
  Build the prompt using the information currently available.

• Switch to fast mode
  Compile immediately with no further questions.

The explanation should be written for a normal user, not in terms of gates, tiers, runtime halts, binding, or other internal concepts.

2. Remove "Settled:" from the diagnosis section.

The AI already resolved those internally.

The user mainly needs to know:

- what still needs input
- why these questions are being asked

I do not think showing everything already resolved provides enough value to justify the space.

Prefer something closer to:

[STRUCTURAL DIAGNOSIS]

Open:
• reviewer authority
• assessment format
• exception routing

Reason:
These items affect how final decisions are made.

3. Keep lightweight headers.

I still want a visible structure such as:

[STRUCTURAL DIAGNOSIS]

[QUESTIONS]

[SPECIFICATION SUFFICIENT]

[OTHER ACTIONS AVAILABLE]

The headers make the interaction easier to scan.

4. For questions, separate the question from the options.

The question should stand on its own.

The options are examples, not the question itself.

Bad:

"What model should be used?
A...
B...
C..."

because the options define the question.

Better:

"What should happen when reviewers disagree?"

A. ...
B. ...
C. ...

The question should remain meaningful even if every option is removed.

5. Every multiple-choice question should visibly allow a custom answer.

Not hidden in reply instructions.

Something like:

D. I'll provide my own answer

or

D. Something else → describe it

This helps prevent menu anchoring and reminds the user that the options are examples rather than constraints.

6. Option letters should be directly selectable.

If only one question is displayed:

A

should be treated as selecting option A.

If multiple questions exist:

1A 2C 3B

remains appropriate.

7. Reduce diagnosis verbosity.

The current diagnosis section is still carrying traces of the old pillar-ledger mindset.

Focus on:

- what remains open
- why it matters

and nothing else unless necessary.

The goal is to reduce answer effort and cognitive load, not just typing effort.

---

At this point I do not want another architecture discussion.

Assume the engine and internal logic remain unchanged.

I want you to generate a complete copy-pasteable revision of the Perfection prompt that implements the revised presentation layer only.

Do not rewrite the underlying mechanics.

Do not redesign the architecture.

Change only:

- user-visible diagnostic output
- question presentation
- option presentation
- sufficiency presentation
- action presentation
- reply handling UX

After generating the revised prompt, list any remaining UX concerns you still have.
